{"type":"message","id":"6727fae2","parentId":"4fab0c5d","timestamp":"2026-03-04T02:59:34.128Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Wed 2026-03-04 10:59 GMT+8] [Subagent Context] You are running as a subagent (depth 1/2). Results auto-announce to your requester; do not busy-poll for status.\n\n[Subagent Task]: ## 任务：继续开发像素编辑器\n\n### 当前状态\n项目已完成大部分开发，但有一些小问题需要修复。\n\n**工作目录**: `/home/one/projects/PixelArtEditor`\n\n### 已完成的文件\n```\n/home/one/projects/PixelArtEditor/\n├── src/\n│   ├── crdt/\n│   │   ├── LWWRegister.ts ✅\n│   │   ├── LWWMap.ts ✅\n│   │   └── PixelData.ts ✅\n│   ├── editor/\n│   │   └── PixelEditor.ts ✅\n│   ├── encoding/\n│   │   ├── Encoder.ts ⚠️ 有小bug\n│   │   └── Decoder.ts ⚠️ 有小bug\n│   └── types.ts ✅\n├── tests/\n│   ├── LWWRegister.test.ts ✅\n│   ├── LWWMap.test.ts ✅\n│   ├── PixelData.test.ts ✅\n│   └── encoding.test.ts ⚠️ 3个测试失败\n├── index.html ✅\n├── main.ts ✅\n├── style.css ✅\n├── package.json ✅\n└── tsconfig.json ✅\n```\n\n### 测试结果\n- ✅ LWWRegister.test.ts: 全部通过\n- ✅ LWWMap.test.ts: 全部通过\n- ✅ PixelData.test.ts: 全部通过\n- ⚠️ encoding.test.ts: 3个测试失败\n\n### 失败的测试\n```\nFAIL tests/encoding.test.ts\n  ● simple state encodes and decodes correctly\n    Expected: [255, 0, 0]\n    Received: null\n\n  ● handles blank pixels (missing keys)\n    Expected: [255, 0, 0]\n    Received: null\n\n  ● large state with many pixels\n    Expected: [125, 125, 255]\n    Received: [100, 125, 255]\n```\n\n### 需要修复的问题\n\n**Decoder.ts 中的问题：**\n1. Palette 解析：Encoder 跳过了 index 0（保留给已删除像素），但 Decoder 把前3个字节当作颜色读进去了\n2. DataView 使用：`data.buffer` 指向整个 ArrayBuffer，而不是当前 chunk 的数据\n\n### 你的任务\n\n1. **修复 encoding 模块的 bug**\n   - 检查 `src/encoding/Encoder.ts` 和 `Decoder.ts`\n   - 修复 palette 索引问题\n   - 修复 DataView 的使用\n\n2. **运行测试验证**\n   ```bash\n   cd /home/one/projects/PixelArtEditor\n   npm test\n   ```\n\n3. **确保所有测试通过**\n\n4. **启动开发服务器验证功能**\n   ```bash\n   npm run dev\n   ```\n\n### 报告要求\n完成后输出报告到 `$HOME/.openclaw-reports/leader-agent/`"}],"timestamp":1772593174126}}
{"type":"message","id":"d2a41d22","parentId":"6727fae2","timestamp":"2026-03-04T02:59:41.334Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to fix bugs in a pixel editor project. Let me start by reading the Encoder.ts and Decoder.ts files to understand the issues, then fix them.\n\nThe issues mentioned are:\n1. Palette parsing: Encoder skips index 0 (reserved for deleted pixels), but Decoder reads the first 3 bytes as a color\n2. DataView usage: `data.buffer` points to the entire ArrayBuffer, not the current chunk's data\n\nLet me first read the files to understand the current implementation.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-c04be865ba9c4fb194afd180c580a29e","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/PixelArtEditor/src/encoding/Encoder.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-3896ff7a16d0421c80ee35d0ba59a5e1","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/PixelArtEditor/src/encoding/Decoder.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-6bc4ef6b263941119bd8fdf4d5e74685","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/PixelArtEditor/tests/encoding.test.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":13580,"output":272,"cacheRead":3426,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":17278,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1772593174128}}
{"type":"message","id":"5cc0749e","parentId":"d2a41d22","timestamp":"2026-03-04T02:59:41.344Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-c04be865ba9c4fb194afd180c580a29e","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import type { PixelDataState, RGB } from \"../types.js\";\n\n/**\n * Binary format constants\n */\nconst MAGIC = \"CRDT\"; // Magic number (4 bytes)\nconst CHUNK_INFO = \"INFO\"; // Info chunk identifier\nconst CHUNK_UUID = \"UUID\"; // UUID chunk identifier\nconst CHUNK_PLTT = \"PLTT\"; // Palette chunk identifier\nconst CHUNK_DATA = \"DATA\"; // Data chunk identifier\n\n/**\n * Data item types in run-length encoding\n */\nconst TAG_BLANK = 0; // Blank pixel (followed by run length)\nconst TAG_PIXEL = 1; // Colored pixel\n\n/**\n * Encoder - Converts PixelData state to binary format\n * Implements the 98% compression pipeline:\n * 1. UUID table deduplication\n * 2. Palette table deduplication  \n * 3. Run-length encoding\n * 4. Binary encoding\n */\nexport class Encoder {\n  #width: number;\n  #height: number;\n  #uuidTable: Map<string, number> = new Map();\n  #paletteTable: Map<string, number> = new Map();\n\n  constructor(width: number, height: number) {\n    this.#width = width;\n    this.#height = height;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Encode state to binary Uint8Array\n   */\n  encode(state: PixelDataState): Uint8Array {\n    // Build lookup tables\n    this.#buildTables(state);\n\n    const chunks: Uint8Array[] = [];\n\n    // Info chunk\n    chunks.push(this.#encodeInfoChunk());\n\n    // UUID chunk\n    chunks.push(this.#encodeUUIDChunk());\n\n    // Palette chunk (skip index 0 for erased pixels)\n    chunks.push(this.#encodePaletteChunk());\n\n    // Data chunk (run-length encoded)\n    chunks.push(this.#encodeDataChunk(state));\n\n    // Concatenate all chunks\n    const totalSize = chunks.reduce((sum, chunk) => sum + chunk.length, 0);\n    const result = new Uint8Array(totalSize);\n    let offset = 0;\n    for (const chunk of chunks) {\n      result.set(chunk, offset);\n      offset += chunk.length;\n    }\n\n    return result;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Build UUID and palette lookup tables from state\n   */\n  #buildTables(state: PixelDataState): void {\n    let uuidIndex = 0;\n    let paletteIndex = 1; // Start from 1, keep 0 for erased pixels\n\n    for (const key in state) {\n      const [peerId, , value] = state[key];\n\n      // Add to UUID table\n      if (!this.#uuidTable.has(peerId)) {\n        this.#uuidTable.set(peerId, uuidIndex++);\n      }\n\n      // Add to palette table (skip null/erased)\n      if (value) {\n        const hex = this.#rgbToHex(value);\n        if (!this.#paletteTable.has(hex)) {\n          this.#paletteTable.set(hex, paletteIndex++);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Encode INFO chunk: magic + width + height\n   */\n  #encodeInfoChunk(): Uint8Array {\n    const data = new Uint8Array(4 + 2 + 2); // 4 bytes magic + 2 bytes width + 2 bytes height\n    const view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n\n    // Magic number\n    data[0] = MAGIC.charCodeAt(0);\n    data[1] = MAGIC.charCodeAt(1);\n    data[2] = MAGIC.charCodeAt(2);\n    data[3] = MAGIC.charCodeAt(3);\n\n    // Width and height (big-endian uint16)\n    view.setUint16(4, this.#width);\n    view.setUint16(6, this.#height);\n\n    return data;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Encode UUID chunk: header + UUIDs\n   */\n  #encodeUUIDChunk(): Uint8Array {\n    const uuids = Array.from(this.#uuidTable.entries())\n      .sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1])\n      .map(([uuid]) => uuid);\n\n    // Remove hyphens from UUIDs (36 chars -> 32 chars)\n    const uuidData = new Uint8Array(uuids.length * 16);\n    for (let i = 0; i < uuids.length; i++) {\n      const clean = uuids[i].replace(/-/g, \"\");\n      for (let j = 0; j < 32; j += 2) {\n        const byte = parseInt(clean.substring(j, j + 2), 16);\n        uuidData[i * 16 + j / 2] = byte;\n      }\n    }\n\n    // Chunk: header (4 + 2) + data\n    const header = this.#encodeChunkHeader(CHUNK_UUID, uuidData.length);\n    const chunk = new Uint8Array(header.length + uuidData.length);\n    chunk.set(header, 0);\n    chunk.set(uuidData, header.length);\n\n    return chunk;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Encode palette chunk: header + colors (skip index 0)\n   */\n  #encodePaletteChunk(): Uint8Array {\n    const colors = Array.from(this.#paletteTable.entries())\n      .sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1])\n      .map(([hex]) => this.#hexToRgb(hex));\n\n    // Skip index 0 by adding dummy bytes\n    const paletteData = new Uint8Array(3 + colors.length * 3);\n    paletteData[0] = 0; // Dummy R\n    paletteData[1] = 0; // Dummy G\n    paletteData[2] = 0; // Dummy B\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < colors.length; i++) {\n      const offset = 3 + i * 3;\n      paletteData[offset] = colors[i][0];\n      paletteData[offset + 1] = colors[i][1];\n      paletteData[offset + 2] = colors[i][2];\n    }\n\n    const header = this.#encodeChunkHeader(CHUNK_PLTT, paletteData.length);\n    const chunk = new Uint8Array(header.length + paletteData.length);\n    chunk.set(header, 0);\n    chunk.set(paletteData, header.length);\n\n    return chunk;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Encode data chunk with run-length encoding\n   */\n  #encodeDataChunk(state: PixelDataState): Uint8Array {\n    const data: number[] = [];\n\n    let x = 0;\n    let y = 0;\n\n    // Iterate through all pixels in row-major order\n    while (y < this.#height) {\n      const key = `${x},${y}`;\n      const pixel = state[key];\n\n      if (!pixel) {\n        // Blank pixel - count consecutive blanks\n        let blankCount = 0;\n        while (y < this.#height && !state[`${x},${y}`]) {\n          blankCount++;\n          x++;\n          if (x >= this.#width) {\n            x = 0;\n            y++;\n          }\n        }\n\n        // Write blank run (max 65535 blanks per run)\n        while (blankCount > 0) {\n          const count = Math.min(blankCount, 65535);\n          data.push(TAG_BLANK);\n          data.push((count >> 8) & 0xff);\n          data.push(count & 0xff);\n          blankCount -= count;\n        }\n      } else {\n        // Non-blank pixel\n        const [peerId, timestamp, value] = pixel;\n        const writerId = this.#uuidTable.get(peerId)!;\n        const colorId = value\n          ? this.#paletteTable.get(this.#rgbToHex(value))!\n          : 0;\n\n        data.push(TAG_PIXEL);\n        data.push(writerId);\n        data.push(timestamp);\n        data.push(colorId);\n\n        x++;\n        if (x >= this.#width) {\n          x = 0;\n          y++;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    const dataArray = new Uint8Array(data);\n    const header = this.#encodeChunkHeader(CHUNK_DATA, dataArray.length);\n    const chunk = new Uint8Array(header.length + dataArray.length);\n    chunk.set(header, 0);\n    chunk.set(dataArray, header.length);\n\n    return chunk;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Encode chunk header: 4-byte identifier + 2-byte length\n   */\n  #encodeChunkHeader(id: string, length: number): Uint8Array {\n    const header = new Uint8Array(6);\n    const view = new DataView(header.buffer);\n\n    header[0] = id.charCodeAt(0);\n    header[1] = id.charCodeAt(1);\n    header[2] = id.charCodeAt(2);\n    header[3] = id.charCodeAt(3);\n    view.setUint16(4, length);\n\n    return header;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Convert RGB to hex string\n   */\n  #rgbToHex(rgb: RGB): string {\n    return (\n      ((rgb[0] << 16) | (rgb[1] << 8) | rgb[2])\n        .toString(16)\n        .padStart(6, \"0\")\n    );\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Convert hex string to RGB\n   */\n  #hexToRgb(hex: string): RGB {\n    const num = parseInt(hex, 16);\n    return [(num >> 16) & 0xff, (num >> 8) & 0xff, num & 0xff];\n  }\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772593181342}}
{"type":"message","id":"5e3ab8d7","parentId":"5cc0749e","timestamp":"2026-03-04T02:59:41.350Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-3896ff7a16d0421c80ee35d0ba59a5e1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import type { PixelDataState, RGB } from \"../types.js\";\n\n/**\n * Decoder - Converts binary format to PixelData state\n */\nexport class Decoder {\n  #width = 0;\n  #height = 0;\n  #uuidTable: string[] = [];\n  #paletteTable: RGB[] = [];\n\n  /**\n   * Decode binary data to state\n   */\n  decode(data: Uint8Array): { state: PixelDataState; width: number; height: number } {\n    let offset = 0;\n\n    // Read magic number\n    const magic = String.fromCharCode(\n      data[offset],\n      data[offset + 1],\n      data[offset + 2],\n      data[offset + 3]\n    );\n    if (magic !== \"CRDT\") {\n      throw new Error(\"Invalid magic number\");\n    }\n    offset += 4;\n\n    // Read width and height\n    const view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n    this.#width = view.getUint16(offset);\n    this.#height = view.getUint16(offset + 2);\n    offset += 4;\n\n    // Read chunks\n    while (offset < data.length) {\n      const chunkId = String.fromCharCode(\n        data[offset],\n        data[offset + 1],\n        data[offset + 2],\n        data[offset + 3]\n      );\n      const chunkLength = view.getUint16(offset + 4);\n      offset += 6;\n\n      const chunkData = data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength);\n      offset += chunkLength;\n\n      switch (chunkId) {\n        case \"UUID\":\n          this.#decodeUUIDChunk(chunkData);\n          break;\n        case \"PLTT\":\n          this.#decodePaletteChunk(chunkData);\n          break;\n        case \"DATA\":\n          return {\n            state: this.#decodeDataChunk(chunkData),\n            width: this.#width,\n            height: this.#height,\n          };\n        default:\n          throw new Error(`Unknown chunk: ${chunkId}`);\n      }\n    }\n\n    throw new Error(\"Missing DATA chunk\");\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Decode UUID chunk\n   */\n  #decodeUUIDChunk(data: Uint8Array): void {\n    const uuidCount = data.length / 16;\n    this.#uuidTable = [];\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < uuidCount; i++) {\n      const offset = i * 16;\n      let hex = \"\";\n      for (let j = 0; j < 16; j++) {\n        hex += data[offset + j].toString(16).padStart(2, \"0\");\n      }\n      // Add hyphens back\n      const uuid = [\n        hex.substring(0, 8),\n        hex.substring(8, 12),\n        hex.substring(12, 16),\n        hex.substring(16, 20),\n        hex.substring(20),\n      ].join(\"-\");\n      this.#uuidTable.push(uuid);\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Decode palette chunk\n   * Note: First 3 bytes are dummy (index 0 reserved for erased pixels)\n   */\n  #decodePaletteChunk(data: Uint8Array): void {\n    // Skip first 3 bytes (dummy at index 0)\n    const colorCount = (data.length - 3) / 3;\n    this.#paletteTable = [null as unknown as RGB]; // index 0 = null/erased\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < colorCount; i++) {\n      const offset = 3 + i * 3; // Skip first 3 bytes\n      this.#paletteTable.push([\n        data[offset],\n        data[offset + 1],\n        data[offset + 2],\n      ]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Decode data chunk\n   */\n  #decodeDataChunk(data: Uint8Array): PixelDataState {\n    const state: PixelDataState = {};\n    let offset = 0;\n    let x = 0;\n    let y = 0;\n    const view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n\n    while (y < this.#height && offset < data.length) {\n      const tag = data[offset];\n\n      if (tag === TAG_BLANK) {\n        // Blank run\n        const count = view.getUint16(offset + 1);\n        offset += 3;\n\n        // Skip blank pixels\n        for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n          x++;\n          if (x >= this.#width) {\n            x = 0;\n            y++;\n          }\n        }\n      } else if (tag === TAG_PIXEL) {\n        // Pixel\n        const writerId = data[offset + 1];\n        const timestamp = data[offset + 2];\n        const colorId = data[offset + 3];\n        offset += 4;\n\n        const key = `${x},${y}`;\n        const peerId = this.#uuidTable[writerId];\n        const color = colorId === 0 ? null : this.#paletteTable[colorId];\n\n        state[key] = [peerId, timestamp, color];\n\n        x++;\n        if (x >= this.#width) {\n          x = 0;\n          y++;\n        }\n      } else {\n        throw new Error(`Invalid tag: ${tag}`);\n      }\n    }\n\n    return state;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Set height (deprecated, now read from INFO chunk)\n   */\n  setHeight(height: number): void {\n    this.#height = height;\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Data item types in run-length encoding\n */\nconst TAG_BLANK = 0;\nconst TAG_PIXEL = 1;"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772593181348}}
{"type":"message","id":"12e24d0a","parentId":"5e3ab8d7","timestamp":"2026-03-04T02:59:41.356Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-6bc4ef6b263941119bd8fdf4d5e74685","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { Encoder, Decoder } from \"../src/encoding/index.js\";\nimport type { PixelDataState, RGB } from \"../src/types.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Encoder/Decoder\", () => {\n  describe(\"roundtrip encoding\", () => {\n    test(\"simple state encodes and decodes correctly\", () => {\n      const state: PixelDataState = {\n        \"0,0\": [\"alice-peer-id\", 1, [255, 0, 0]],\n        \"1,0\": [\"alice-peer-id\", 2, [0, 255, 0]],\n        \"0,1\": [\"bob-peer-id\", 1, [0, 0, 255]],\n      };\n\n      const encoder = new Encoder(2, 2);\n      const decoder = new Decoder();\n      decoder.setHeight(2);\n\n      const encoded = encoder.encode(state);\n      const { state: decoded } = decoder.decode(encoded);\n\n      // Check that all keys are present\n      expect(Object.keys(decoded)).toEqual([\"0,0\", \"1,0\", \"0,1\"]);\n\n      // Check values (peer IDs may differ, but values should be the same)\n      expect(decoded[\"0,0\"][2]).toEqual([255, 0, 0]);\n      expect(decoded[\"1,0\"][2]).toEqual([0, 255, 0]);\n      expect(decoded[\"0,1\"][2]).toEqual([0, 0, 255]);\n    });\n\n    test(\"handles null (erased) pixels\", () => {\n      const state: PixelDataState = {\n        \"0,0\": [\"alice\", 1, [255, 0, 0]],\n        \"1,0\": [\"alice\", 2, null], // Erased\n        \"0,1\": [\"bob\", 1, [0, 0, 255]],\n      };\n\n      const encoder = new Encoder(2, 2);\n      const decoder = new Decoder();\n      decoder.setHeight(2);\n\n      const encoded = encoder.encode(state);\n      const { state: decoded } = decoder.decode(encoded);\n\n      expect(decoded[\"1,0\"][2]).toBeNull();\n    });\n\n    test(\"handles blank pixels (missing keys)\", () => {\n      const state: PixelDataState = {\n        \"5,5\": [\"alice\", 1, [255, 0, 0]],\n      };\n\n      const encoder = new Encoder(10, 10);\n      const decoder = new Decoder();\n      decoder.setHeight(10);\n\n      const encoded = encoder.encode(state);\n      const { state: decoded } = decoder.decode(encoded);\n\n      expect(decoded[\"5,5\"][2]).toEqual([255, 0, 0]);\n    });\n\n    test(\"large state with many pixels\", () => {\n      const state: PixelDataState = {};\n      for (let y = 0; y < 10; y++) {\n        for (let x = 0; x < 10; x++) {\n          state[`${x},${y}`] = [\"alice\", 1, [x * 25, y * 25, 255]];\n        }\n      }\n\n      const encoder = new Encoder(10, 10);\n      const decoder = new Decoder();\n      decoder.setHeight(10);\n\n      const encoded = encoder.encode(state);\n      const { state: decoded } = decoder.decode(encoded);\n\n      expect(Object.keys(decoded).length).toBe(100);\n      expect(decoded[\"5,5\"][2]).toEqual([125, 125, 255]);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe(\"compression ratio\", () => {\n    test(\"binary encoding is smaller than JSON\", () => {\n      const state: PixelDataState = {};\n      for (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) {\n        const x = Math.floor(Math.random() * 10);\n        const y = Math.floor(Math.random() * 10);\n        state[`${x},${y}`] = [\"alice\", i + 1, [Math.random() * 255, Math.random() * 255, Math.random() * 255]];\n      }\n\n      const jsonSize = JSON.stringify(state).length;\n      const encoder = new Encoder(10, 10);\n      const encoded = encoder.encode(state);\n\n      expect(encoded.length).toBeLessThan(jsonSize);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe(\"run-length encoding\", () => {\n    test(\"encodes consecutive identical timestamps efficiently\", () => {\n      const state: PixelDataState = {};\n      for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {\n        state[`${i},0`] = [\"alice\", 1, [255, 0, 0]];\n      }\n\n      const encoder = new Encoder(20, 1);\n      const decoder = new Decoder();\n      decoder.setHeight(1);\n\n      const encoded = encoder.encode(state);\n      const { state: decoded } = decoder.decode(encoded);\n\n      // Should successfully decode all 20 pixels\n      expect(Object.keys(decoded).length).toBe(20);\n      for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {\n        expect(decoded[`${i},0`]).toBeDefined();\n      }\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe(\"error handling\", () => {\n    test(\"throws on invalid magic number\", () => {\n      const decoder = new Decoder();\n      const invalidData = new Uint8Array([0, 0, 0, 0, 10, 0]);\n\n      expect(() => decoder.decode(invalidData)).toThrow(\"Invalid magic number\");\n    });\n\n    test(\"throws on unknown chunk\", () => {\n      const decoder = new Decoder();\n      const data = new Uint8Array(16);\n\n      // Set magic number\n      data[0] = 67; // C\n      data[1] = 82; // R\n      data[2] = 68; // D\n      data[3] = 84; // T\n\n      // Set width\n      data[4] = 0;\n      data[5] = 10;\n\n      // Invalid chunk header\n      data[6] = 88; // X\n      data[7] = 88; // X\n      data[8] = 88; // X\n      data[9] = 88; // X\n      data[10] = 0;\n      data[11] = 0;\n\n      expect(() => decoder.decode(data)).toThrow(\"Unknown chunk\");\n    });\n  });\n});"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772593181354}}
{"type":"message","id":"8d47f824","parentId":"12e24d0a","timestamp":"2026-03-04T03:02:09.670Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the issues. Let me analyze the problems:\n\n1. **DataView issue in Decoder**: The Decoder uses `new DataView(data.buffer)` but `data.buffer` points to the entire ArrayBuffer, not just the current chunk. When `data` is a slice of a larger buffer, `data.buffer` still refers to the original buffer, and `data.byteOffset` would be needed to correctly access the data.\n\n2. **Palette index issue**: Looking at the Encoder:\n   - Palette indices start from 1 (index 0 is reserved for erased pixels)\n   - Encoder adds dummy 3 bytes at the start of palette data (paletteData[0,1,2] = 0)\n   - Then adds colors starting from offset 3\n\n   Looking at the Decoder:\n   - It correctly skips the first 3 bytes: `const colorCount = (data.length - 3) / 3;`\n   - But wait, it initializes `#paletteTable = [null as unknown as RGB]` which should be correct\n\nLet me trace through more carefully:\n\nIn Encoder:\n```javascript\n#encodePaletteChunk(): Uint8Array {\n  const colors = Array.from(this.#paletteTable.entries())\n    .sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1])\n    .map(([hex]) => this.#hexToRgb(hex));\n\n  // Skip index 0 by adding dummy bytes\n  const paletteData = new Uint8Array(3 + colors.length * 3);\n  paletteData[0] = 0; // Dummy R\n  paletteData[1] = 0; // Dummy G\n  paletteData[2] = 0; // Dummy B\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < colors.length; i++) {\n    const offset = 3 + i * 3;\n    paletteData[offset] = colors[i][0];\n    paletteData[offset + 1] = colors[i][1];\n    paletteData[offset + 2] = colors[i][2];\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nSo if we have 1 color [255, 0, 0] at index 1, paletteData would be:\n- [0, 0, 0, 255, 0, 0]\n- That's 6 bytes total (3 dummy + 3 for the actual color)\n\nIn Decoder:\n```javascript\n#decodePaletteChunk(data: Uint8Array): void {\n  // Skip first 3 bytes (dummy at index 0)\n  const colorCount = (data.length - 3) / 3;\n  this.#paletteTable = [null as unknown as RGB]; // index 0 = null/erased\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < colorCount; i++) {\n    const offset = 3 + i * 3; // Skip first 3 bytes\n    this.#paletteTable.push([\n      data[offset],\n      data[offset + 1],\n      data[offset + 2],\n    ]);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWith data = [0, 0, 0, 255, 0, 0]:\n- colorCount = (6 - 3) / 3 = 1\n- paletteTable = [null]\n- Loop: i=0, offset = 3, push [255, 0, 0]\n- paletteTable = [null, [255, 0, 0]]\n\nThis looks correct!\n\nSo the issue must be with the DataView. Let me check the DataView usage in the Decoder:\n\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n```\n\nThis is the problem! `data.buffer` is the underlying ArrayBuffer of the entire data, but `data` might be a slice. For example, when `chunkData = data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength)` is called, `chunkData.buffer` is the same as `data.buffer`, but `chunkData.byteOffset` tells us where in that buffer the data actually starts.\n\nSo when we do `view.getUint16(offset + 1)`, we're reading from the wrong position!\n\nThe fix is to use:\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer, data.byteOffset, data.length);\n```\n\nOr alternatively:\n```javascript\nconst count = (data[offset + 1] << 8) | data[offset + 2];\n```\n\nLet me check all places where DataView is used:\n\n1. In `decode()`:\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n// Used to read width/height:\nthis.#width = view.getUint16(offset); // offset = 4, reading bytes 4-5\nthis.#height = view.getUint16(offset + 2); // reading bytes 6-7\n// Used to read chunk lengths:\nconst chunkLength = view.getUint16(offset + 4);\n```\n\n2. In `#decodeDataChunk()`:\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n// Used to read blank run count:\nconst count = view.getUint16(offset + 1);\n```\n\nThe main `decode()` method uses the original `data` which starts at offset 0, so `data.buffer` is fine there. But `#decodeDataChunk(data)` receives a slice of the data, so it has a different `byteOffset`.\n\nActually wait, let me look at this more carefully. In `decode()`:\n```javascript\nconst chunkData = data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength);\n```\n\n`data.slice()` creates a new Uint8Array view. The new Uint8Array has its own buffer that starts at the right position. Actually, I need to verify this...\n\nActually no! `Uint8Array.prototype.slice()` creates a **copy** of the data, not a view. So `chunkData.buffer` would be a new ArrayBuffer containing only the chunk data, and `chunkData.byteOffset` would be 0.\n\nLet me verify this... Actually, `TypedArray.prototype.slice()` does create a copy. So `chunkData.buffer` would be correct.\n\nHmm, let me think again. What about the test failures?\n\nLet's trace through the \"simple state encodes and decodes correctly\" test:\n\nState:\n```javascript\n{\n  \"0,0\": [\"alice-peer-id\", 1, [255, 0, 0]],\n  \"1,0\": [\"alice-peer-id\", 2, [0, 255, 0]],\n  \"0,1\": [\"bob-peer-id\", 1, [0, 0, 255]],\n}\n```\n\nAfter encoding:\n- UUID table: {\"alice-peer-id\": 0, \"bob-peer-id\": 1}\n- Palette table: {\"ff0000\": 1, \"00ff00\": 2, \"0000ff\": 3}\n\nWait, let me check the hex function:\n```javascript\n#rgbToHex(rgb: RGB): string {\n  return (\n    ((rgb[0] << 16) | (rgb[1] << 8) | rgb[2])\n      .toString(16)\n      .padStart(6, \"0\")\n  );\n}\n```\n\nFor [255, 0, 0]: (255 << 16) | (0 << 8) | 0 = 16711680 = 0xff0000 = \"ff0000\" ✓\n\nSo palette should be:\n- Index 0: null (erased)\n- Index 1: [255, 0, 0]\n- Index 2: [0, 255, 0]\n- Index 3: [0, 0, 255]\n\nWhen encoding the data chunk:\n- Pixel at (0,0): writerId=0 (alice), timestamp=1, colorId=1 (red)\n- Pixel at (1,0): writerId=0 (alice), timestamp=2, colorId=2 (green)\n- Pixel at (0,1): writerId=1 (bob), timestamp=1, colorId=3 (blue)\n\nData chunk should be:\n- TAG_PIXEL(1), writerId(0), timestamp(1), colorId(1) for (0,0)\n- TAG_PIXEL(1), writerId(0), timestamp(2), colorId(2) for (1,0)\n- TAG_PIXEL(1), writerId(1), timestamp(1), colorId(3) for (0,1)\n\nWait, but the canvas is 2x2 = 4 pixels. The state only has 3 pixels defined. So there should be a blank at (1,1).\n\nActually, let me re-read the encoder data chunk logic:\n\n```javascript\nwhile (y < this.#height) {\n  const key = `${x},${y}`;\n  const pixel = state[key];\n\n  if (!pixel) {\n    // Blank pixel - count consecutive blanks\n    let blankCount = 0;\n    while (y < this.#height && !state[`${x},${y}`]) {\n      blankCount++;\n      x++;\n      if (x >= this.#width) {\n        x = 0;\n        y++;\n      }\n    }\n    // ...\n  } else {\n    // ...\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor the 2x2 grid with pixels at (0,0), (1,0), (0,1):\n- x=0, y=0: state[\"0,0\"] exists → write pixel\n- x=1, y=0: state[\"1,0\"] exists → write pixel\n- x=0, y=1: state[\"0,1\"] exists → write pixel\n- x=1, y=1: state[\"1,1\"] doesn't exist → blank run of 1\n\nSo data should be:\n1. TAG_PIXEL, 0, 1, 1 (for 0,0)\n2. TAG_PIXEL, 0, 2, 2 (for 1,0)\n3. TAG_PIXEL, 1, 1, 3 (for 0,1)\n4. TAG_BLANK, 0, 1 (for 1,1)\n\nThat's 4 + 4 + 4 + 3 = 15 bytes.\n\nNow let's trace through the decoder:\n\nAfter reading info chunk:\n- width = 2, height = 2\n\nAfter reading UUID chunk:\n- uuidTable[0] = \"alice-peer-id\"\n- uuidTable[1] = \"bob-peer-id\"\n\nAfter reading palette chunk (should be 12 bytes: 3 dummy + 3*3 colors):\n- paletteTable[0] = null\n- paletteTable[1] = [255, 0, 0]\n- paletteTable[2] = [0, 255, 0]\n- paletteTable[3] = [0, 0, 255]\n\nNow the data chunk. Let me check if there's an issue with how the data chunk is decoded.\n\nActually, I see a potential issue! In `#decodeDataChunk`:\n\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n```\n\nIf `data` is the chunkData that was created by `data.slice()`, then `data.buffer` would be the full original buffer, not just the chunk data!\n\nWait, but I said `TypedArray.prototype.slice()` creates a copy... Let me verify.\n\nActually, looking at MDN: \"The slice() method returns a shallow copy of a portion of a typed array into a new typed array object.\"\n\nSo `slice()` does create a new typed array, but what about the underlying buffer?\n\nAccording to the spec, when you call `slice()` on a TypedArray:\n1. It creates a new ArrayBuffer\n2. It copies the elements into the new buffer\n3. It returns a new TypedArray backed by the new buffer\n\nSo `chunkData.buffer` should be the new, smaller buffer, and `chunkData.byteOffset` should be 0.\n\nHmm, but the tests are failing. Let me think about what else could be wrong.\n\nOh wait! I see another potential issue. Look at the Encoder's `#encodePaletteChunk`:\n\n```javascript\n#encodePaletteChunk(): Uint8Array {\n  const colors = Array.from(this.#paletteTable.entries())\n    .sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1])\n    .map(([hex]) => this.#hexToRgb(hex));\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe palette table starts at index 1, but when we sort by index and map, we get colors starting from index 0 in the array `colors`. Then we add 3 dummy bytes at the start.\n\nSo if `#paletteTable` has:\n```\n\"ff0000\" -> 1\n\"00ff00\" -> 2\n\"0000ff\" -> 3\n```\n\nAfter sorting and mapping:\n```\ncolors = [[255, 0, 0], [0, 255, 0], [0, 0, 255]]\n```\n\nThen we create:\n```\npaletteData = [0, 0, 0, 255, 0, 0, 0, 255, 0, 0, 0, 255]\n```\n\nThat's 3 dummy bytes + 3 colors * 3 bytes = 12 bytes.\n\nIn the decoder:\n```javascript\nconst colorCount = (data.length - 3) / 3; // (12 - 3) / 3 = 3\n```\n\nSo we'd read 3 colors correctly.\n\nBut wait, let me check the test again:\n\n```javascript\ntest(\"handles blank pixels (missing keys)\", () => {\n  const state: PixelDataState = {\n    \"5,5\": [\"alice\", 1, [255, 0, 0]],\n  };\n\n  const encoder = new Encoder(10, 10);\n  const decoder = new Decoder();\n  decoder.setHeight(2);  // <-- This says 2, not 10!\n\n  const encoded = encoder.encode(state);\n  const { state: decoded } = decoder.decode(encoded);\n\n  expect(decoded[\"5,5\"][2]).toEqual([255, 0, 0]);\n});\n```\n\nWait, `decoder.setHeight(2)` but the encoder is `10x10`! This is a bug in the test, but also the setHeight is deprecated and the width/height should come from the INFO chunk.\n\nLet me check... The encoder encodes width=10, height=10. The decoder reads these from the INFO chunk. So the setHeight(2) should be ignored.\n\nBut wait, looking at the decoder:\n```javascript\ndecode(data: Uint8Array): { state: PixelDataState; width: number; height: number } {\n  // ...\n  this.#width = view.getUint16(offset);\n  this.#height = view.getUint16(offset + 2);\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nSo it does read width/height from the INFO chunk. The setHeight is just a deprecated method that sets `this.#height` before decode is called, but decode will override it.\n\nActually, I think I need to just run the tests and see what's happening. Let me do that.\n\nActually wait, I realize there's another issue with DataView. In the main decode() method:\n\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer);\nthis.#width = view.getUint16(offset);  // offset = 4\nthis.#height = view.getUint16(offset + 2);  // offset = 6\n```\n\nThis reads from the correct positions because `data` starts at offset 0 of its buffer.\n\nBut later:\n```javascript\nconst chunkLength = view.getUint16(offset + 4);\n```\n\nThis uses the same `view` which is based on `data.buffer`. If `offset` has moved past 0, this should still work because we're using absolute positions.\n\nWait, actually the issue is clearer now. Let me trace through the DataView issue more carefully:\n\n1. In `decode()`, `data` is the full encoded data. `data.buffer` is the ArrayBuffer backing it. Since `data` is the result of `encoder.encode()` which creates a new Uint8Array, `data.byteOffset` should be 0. So `new DataView(data.buffer)` is fine for the main decode.\n\n2. In `#decodeDataChunk(data)`, `data` is `chunkData` which is created by `data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength)`. According to MDN, `slice()` creates a new TypedArray with a new ArrayBuffer, so `data.byteOffset` should be 0 and `data.buffer` should be a new buffer containing only the chunk data.\n\nSo the DataView should work correctly in both cases...\n\nLet me think about what else could be wrong.\n\nActually, wait! I see the issue now. Look at the DataView in decode():\n\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n// ...\nconst chunkLength = view.getUint16(offset + 4);\n```\n\nBut `offset` at this point is not 0! It has been incremented by 4 (magic) + 4 (width/height) = 8 bytes. So when we read `chunkLength`, we're reading from `offset + 4` which is correct.\n\nBut then look at how we get the chunkData:\n```javascript\nconst chunkData = data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength);\n```\n\nWait, this is wrong! At this point:\n- offset = 8 (after magic + width/height)\n- We read chunk ID from data[offset:offset+4]\n- We read chunk length from view.getUint16(offset+4) - but wait, we already read the chunk ID bytes\n\nLet me trace through more carefully:\n\n```javascript\noffset = 8;  // After magic (4) + width (2) + height (2)\n\n// First iteration (UUID chunk):\nconst chunkId = String.fromCharCode(\n  data[offset],     // data[8]\n  data[offset + 1], // data[9]\n  data[offset + 2], // data[10]\n  data[offset + 3]  // data[11]\n);\nconst chunkLength = view.getUint16(offset + 4);  // bytes 12-13\noffset += 6;  // offset = 14\n\nconst chunkData = data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength);  // starts at 14\noffset += chunkLength;\n```\n\nOK so that's correct. The chunkData starts at offset 14 and has length chunkLength.\n\nLet me think about the actual test failure. The tests show:\n- Expected: [255, 0, 0]\n- Received: null\n\nSo the decoded color is null instead of [255, 0, 0]. This means the palette lookup is failing.\n\nLet me check the palette decoding again...\n\nActually, I think I found the bug! Look at the Decoder:\n\n```javascript\n#decodePaletteChunk(data: Uint8Array): void {\n  // Skip first 3 bytes (dummy at index 0)\n  const colorCount = (data.length - 3) / 3;\n  this.#paletteTable = [null as unknown as RGB]; // index 0 = null/erased\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < colorCount; i++) {\n    const offset = 3 + i * 3; // Skip first 3 bytes\n    this.#paletteTable.push([\n      data[offset],\n      data[offset + 1],\n      data[offset + 2],\n    ]);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis looks correct. But wait, let me check what data is passed to this function:\n\nIn decode():\n```javascript\nconst chunkData = data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength);\n// ...\ncase \"PLTT\":\n  this.#decodePaletteChunk(chunkData);\n  break;\n```\n\nSo chunkData is the palette data including the header... wait no, the header is read separately:\n\n```javascript\nconst chunkId = String.fromCharCode(\n  data[offset],\n  data[offset + 1],\n  data[offset + 2],\n  data[offset + 3]\n);\nconst chunkLength = view.getUint16(offset + 4);\noffset += 6;  // <-- header is skipped\n\nconst chunkData = data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength);\n```\n\nSo chunkData is just the palette data without the header. Good.\n\nHmm, let me look at the test \"large state with many pixels\":\n```javascript\nexpect(decoded[\"5,5\"][2]).toEqual([125, 125, 255]);\n```\n\nBut received: [100, 125, 255]\n\nSo x=5 should give 5*25=125, y=5 should give 5*25=125, and z=255. So [125, 125, 255] is expected.\n\nBut we got [100, 125, 255]. 100 = 4*25, so it seems like the x coordinate is off by 1?\n\nWait, let me check the encoding of colors:\n```javascript\nfor (let x = 0; x < 10; x++) {\n  for (let y = 0; y < 10; y++) {\n    state[`${x},${y}`] = [\"alice\", 1, [x * 25, y * 25, 255]];\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSo:\n- (0,0) -> [0, 0, 255]\n- (1,0) -> [25, 0, 255]\n- (5,5) -> [125, 125, 255]\n\nLet me check the hex encoding:\n- [0, 0, 255] -> 0x0000ff = \"0000ff\"\n- [25, 0, 255] -> 0x1900ff = \"1900ff\"\n- [125, 125, 255] -> 0x7d7dff = \"7d7dff\"\n\nWait, the palette indices depend on the order in which colors are added to the palette table. The table is a Map, so iteration order is insertion order.\n\nFor the 10x10 grid:\n- (0,0) adds [0, 0, 255] -> index 1\n- (1,0) adds [25, 0, 255] -> index 2\n- (2,0) adds [50, 0, 255] -> index 3\n- ...\n- (5,5) adds [125, 125, 255]\n\nBut wait, the state is iterated in insertion order, but the encoder iterates over all pixels in row-major order:\n\n```javascript\n#buildTables(state: PixelDataState): void {\n  let uuidIndex = 0;\n  let paletteIndex = 1; // Start from 1, keep 0 for erased pixels\n\n  for (const key in state) {\n    // ...\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but `for (const key in state)` iterates in insertion order for object keys, not in any particular order. And the state is built with keys like \"5,5\" being added after others.\n\nActually wait, the test builds state with nested loops:\n```javascript\nfor (let y = 0; y < 10; y++) {\n  for (let x = 0; x < 10; x++) {\n    state[`${x},${y}`] = ...\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSo the order is:\n- y=0: x=0,1,2,...,9 -> keys: 0,0; 1,0; 2,0; ...; 9,0\n- y=1: x=0,1,2,...,9 -> keys: 0,1; 1,1; 2,1; ...; 9,1\n- ...\n\nAnd `for (const key in state)` would iterate in this order (insertion order).\n\nSo the palette would be:\n- [0, 0, 255] -> index 1\n- [25, 0, 255] -> index 2\n- [50, 0, 255] -> index 3\n- [75, 0, 255] -> index 4\n- [100, 0, 255] -> index 5\n- [125, 0, 255] -> index 6\n- [150, 0, 255] -> index 7\n- [175, 0, 255] -> index 8\n- [200, 0, 255] -> index 9\n- [225, 0, 255] -> index 10\n- [0, 25, 255] -> index 11\n- [25, 25, 255] -> index 12\n- ...\n\nWait, but all the colors are unique (x*25, y*25, 255). So there would be 100 unique colors in the palette.\n\nNow, when encoding pixel (5,5) with color [125, 125, 255]:\n- This color should have some index in the palette\n- But the encoder iterates in row-major order, so it processes pixels in order:\n  - (0,0), (1,0), (2,0), ..., (9,0), (0,1), (1,1), ..., (9,1), ..., (0,9), ..., (9,9)\n  - Wait, that's not right. The encoder iterates with:\n    ```javascript\n    while (y < this.#height) {\n      const key = `${x},${y}`;\n      // ...\n      x++;\n      if (x >= this.#width) {\n        x = 0;\n        y++;\n      }\n    }\n    ```\n\nSo the encoder iterates in the same order: (0,0), (1,0), ..., (9,0), (0,1), ..., (9,9).\n\nFor pixel (5,5):\n- This is the 5 + 5*10 = 55th pixel (0-indexed)\n- But the state has it at key \"5,5\"\n\nWait, I","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"error","timestamp":1772593181356,"errorMessage":"terminated"}}
{"type":"message","id":"6ef379d3","parentId":"8d47f824","timestamp":"2026-03-04T03:02:51.447Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the issues. Let me analyze the code:\n\n**Problem 1: DataView usage in Decoder**\nIn `#decodeDataChunk`, the code uses:\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer);\nconst count = view.getUint16(offset + 1);\n```\n\nThe issue is that `data.buffer` points to the entire ArrayBuffer, not just the chunk data. When `data` is created via `data.slice(offset, offset + chunkLength)`, it creates a new view but shares the same underlying buffer. So `data.buffer` still points to the original buffer, and `data.byteOffset` tells us where this view starts.\n\nThe fix should be:\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer, data.byteOffset, data.length);\n```\n\nOr better, use the data array directly for reading:\n```javascript\nconst count = (data[offset + 1] << 8) | data[offset + 2];\n```\n\n**Problem 2: Palette parsing**\nLooking at the encoder:\n- It starts paletteIndex at 1 (reserving 0 for erased pixels)\n- It adds 3 dummy bytes at the start of paletteData (for index 0)\n- So paletteData = [0, 0, 0, R1, G1, B1, R2, G2, B2, ...]\n\nLooking at the decoder:\n- It correctly skips the first 3 bytes\n- It calculates colorCount as `(data.length - 3) / 3`\n- It starts with `[null as unknown as RGB]` at index 0\n\nWait, the decoder looks correct. Let me re-read...\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the decoder:\n```javascript\n#decodePaletteChunk(data: Uint8Array): void {\n  // Skip first 3 bytes (dummy at index 0)\n  const colorCount = (data.length - 3) / 3;\n  this.#paletteTable = [null as unknown as RGB]; // index 0 = null/erased\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < colorCount; i++) {\n    const offset = 3 + i * 3; // Skip first 3 bytes\n    this.#paletteTable.push([\n      data[offset],\n      data[offset + 1],\n      data[offset + 2],\n    ]);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis looks correct. The palette table starts with null at index 0, then adds colors starting from index 1.\n\nBut wait, the test says:\n```\nExpected: [255, 0, 0]\nReceived: null\n```\n\nThis means the color is coming back as null. Let me trace through the data chunk decoding...\n\nIn `#decodeDataChunk`:\n```javascript\nconst color = colorId === 0 ? null : this.#paletteTable[colorId];\n```\n\nIf colorId is 0, color is null. If colorId is not 0, it should look up the color in the palette table.\n\nThe issue might be in how the encoder writes the colorId. Let me check...\n\nIn the encoder:\n```javascript\nconst colorId = value\n  ? this.#paletteTable.get(this.#rgbToHex(value))!\n  : 0;\n```\n\nThis looks correct - if value exists, get the colorId from the palette table.\n\nWait, I see the issue now! In the decoder's `#decodeDataChunk`:\n\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n```\n\nThis creates a DataView on the entire buffer, not on the chunk data. When we do:\n```javascript\nconst count = view.getUint16(offset + 1);\n```\n\nWe're reading from the wrong position in the buffer!\n\nThe fix should be:\n```javascript\nconst view = new DataView(data.buffer, data.byteOffset, data.length);\n```\n\nOr we can just read the bytes directly:\n```javascript\nconst count = (data[offset + 1] << 8) | data[offset + 2];\n```\n\nLet me also check the timestamp reading... In the encoder:\n```javascript\ndata.push(timestamp);\n```\n\nThis pushes a single byte, but timestamps could be larger than 255. This is a potential issue, but let's focus on the DataView bug first.\n\nActually, looking at the test:\n```javascript\ntest(\"large state with many pixels\", () => {\n  const state: PixelDataState = {};\n  for (let y = 0; y < 10; y++) {\n    for (let x = 0; x < 10; x++) {\n      state[`${x},${y}`] = [\"alice\", 1, [x * 25, y * 25, 255]];\n    }\n  }\n  ...\n  expect(decoded[\"5,5\"][2]).toEqual([125, 125, 255]);\n});\n```\n\nThe expected color is [125, 125, 255] but received is [100, 125, 255]. The x=5, y=5 should give [125, 125, 255].\n\nWait, 100 = 4 * 25, so x=4. This suggests an off-by-one error somewhere.\n\nLet me trace through more carefully...\n\nActually, I think the main issue is the DataView bug. Let me fix that first and see if it resolves the issues.\n\nLet me also check if there's an issue with the blank run encoding. In the encoder:\n```javascript\ndata.push(TAG_BLANK);\ndata.push((count >> 8) & 0xff);\ndata.push(count & 0xff);\n```\n\nAnd in the decoder:\n```javascript\nconst count = view.getUint16(offset + 1);\n```\n\nIf the DataView is pointing to the wrong buffer, this will read garbage.\n\nLet me fix the Decoder.ts file.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now I understand the issues. Let me analyze the bugs:\n\n**Bug 1: DataView usage in Decoder**\nIn `#decodeDataChunk`, the code uses `new DataView(data.buffer)` which points to the entire ArrayBuffer, not the chunk data. When `data` is a slice, we need to account for `data.byteOffset`.\n\n**Bug 2: Similar issue in the main decode method**\nThe width/height reading also uses `new DataView(data.buffer)` without accounting for offset.\n\nLet me fix the Decoder.ts:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-c90df57de1af487ba2a7a4977a1dc87b","name":"edit","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/PixelArtEditor/src/encoding/Decoder.ts","newText":"// Read width and height\n    const view = new DataView(data.buffer, data.byteOffset, data.length);\n    this.#width = view.getUint16(offset);\n    this.#height = view.getUint16(offset + 2);\n    offset += 4;","oldText":"// Read width and height\n    const view = new DataView(data.buffer);\n    this.#width = view.getUint16(offset);\n    this.#height = view.getUint16(offset + 2);\n    offset += 4;"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":2632,"cacheRead":21534,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":24168,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"t